# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_SMC_CALL */
int64_t smc_call(uint32_t smc, const croi_smc_parameters_t *parameters,
                 croi_smc_result_t *result);
```

# DESCRIPTION

On arm64, `smc_call` makes an SMC Calling Convention call into firmware, through the conduit the FADT names: an SMC instruction, or HVC where firmware runs at EL2.
<smc> is a resource(2) of kind SMC that covers the function id.

The call reads <parameters>:

```c
typedef struct {
  uint32_t func_id;
  uint8_t padding1[4];
  uint64_t arg1, arg2, arg3, arg4, arg5, arg6;
  uint16_t client_id;
  uint16_t secure_os_id;
  uint8_t padding2[4];
} croi_smc_parameters_t;
```

`func_id` goes in w0, `arg1` to `arg6` in x1 to x6, and `secure_os_id << 16 | client_id` in w7.
The results are written to <result>, a `croi_smc_result_t` of `arg0` to `arg3` (x0 to x3) and `arg6` (x6, a session id for some implementations).
<parameters> and <result> must not be null (`CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`).

A resource grants the function ids in its range.
The PSCI calls that change CPU or system state belong to the kernel and no resource grants them: CPU_SUSPEND, CPU_OFF, CPU_ON, MIGRATE, SYSTEM_OFF, SYSTEM_RESET, SYSTEM_SUSPEND and the rest of that family, in either calling convention.
A function id outside the resource is `CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE`; one the kernel keeps is `CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED`; a resource of another kind is `CROI_ERR_WRONG_TYPE`.

With `croi.smccc_qcom=on` on the kernel's command line, a yielding call (bit 31 of the function id clear) follows Qualcomm's resume protocol, one at a time: while x0 says INTERRUPTED (1) the kernel calls again with function id 1 and x6 the session id it returned.

On other architectures the call is `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.

